## 1. What the player sees

### 1.1 Home screen button

- Add an **Online Play** button in the Home menu's first row (`.home-actions-row` in `js/screens/home-screen.js`), **right after the Local play button**, so the row is: Play | Local play | 🌐.
- It is a **square icon button the same size as the Settings button** (`.home-settings`, styled with `.home-utility-buttons > button` in `css/home.css`). Make its width and height match exactly at every breakpoint in `css/responsive.css`. Play and Local play shrink to share the rest of the row. The row must still fit on phones in landscape. Check the existing `ui-consistency` and `stylesheets` tests.
- Its icon is a **globe**: add `globe` to `js/ui/icons.js` in the same stroke style as the other icons (a circle, an equator, two meridian curves). Don't use the emoji itself.
- Accessible name and tooltip: "Online play" (FR: "Jeu en ligne"), via `tattr('aria-label', …)` and `tattr('title', …)`.
- Give it `data-nav`, `data-home-action="online"`, and add it to `this.actions` so keyboard and gamepad menu navigation reach it.

**Enabled and disabled states:**

- It is **enabled whenever the device is online** and a fighter is playable. Use `navigator.onLine` with the `online` / `offline` window events, and update the button live, the same way `syncLocalAction` follows controllers. Put the check in a small module (e.g. `js/core/connectivity.js`) with an `onChange` listener so tests can fake it.
- It is **disabled when offline**, with the reason "You're offline. Connect to the internet to play online." in its `aria-describedby` and on its cell's tooltip, like Local play's `localNote` / `localCell` pattern. With no playable fighter it is disabled like the other match actions (`MATCH_ACTIONS` / the no-fighters note).
- **Enabled: it glows green** exactly like Local play's `.is-ready` (the same border colour, `::after` glow and `home-download-glow` animation). **Disabled because offline: it glows red** with the same animation and shape in a red tone. Add a red token rather than hard-coding the colour twice. Respect reduced motion as Local play does, and pause the animation while `.screen--home[inert]`.
- If the device goes offline while the player is anywhere in Online Play, show a dialog saying the connection was lost and return to Home.

### 1.2 Username (first step, every time)

- Pressing Online Play opens an **Online name** screen (or dialog) before anything else: "Choose a name other players will see."
- Rules: 2 to 16 characters after trimming; letters (any language), digits, spaces, `_` `-` `.` and `'`. No control characters, emoji or HTML. Collapse repeated spaces. The server **re-validates** everything, so never trust the client.
- Remember the last name in the existing settings store (`js/core/settings.js`; bump its version and migrate cleanly) and prefill it next time. The player can always change it.
- Keyboard, touch (on-screen keyboard), and gamepad users must all be able to complete this screen. A gamepad player should be able to confirm a remembered name without typing.

### 1.3 Online lobby

After the name, show the lobby with:

- **Create room** opens a small form with:
  - a **Room name**, prefilled as "{name}'s room" (FR: "Salle de {name}"; use the localization formatter, and handle names ending in "s" sensibly in English, e.g. "Chris's room" is fine). The player can edit it, using the same validation as names, up to 24 characters.
  - **Visibility**: **Public** (listed in the lobby) or **Private** (only joinable with its code).
  - **Mode**: Quick Battle only for now. Show it as a fixed, single option so more modes can be added later without redesigning the screen. Put the list of online modes in config (e.g. `CONFIG.online.modes = ['quick-battle']`).
- **Join with code**: a field for a room code. Codes are 5 characters from an unambiguous alphabet (no 0/O/1/I/L), shown in uppercase and entered case-insensitively.
- **Public rooms list**: room name, host name, mode, players (1/2), and a Join button. It refreshes every ~5 seconds while open (poll, don't keep a socket open just for the list) and has an empty state ("No public rooms right now. Create one!"). Full or in-match rooms are hidden or shown as unjoinable.
- **Back** returns to Home. Every screen uses the existing screen manager and menu navigation, with the same look as the Local Play / Quick Battle setup screens.

### 1.4 Room

- Show the room's name and code. Include a **Copy code** button; itch runs the game in an iframe, so URLs aren't shareable and the code is the way to invite. Handle the Clipboard API being blocked in the iframe by selecting the code text instead.
- Show both player slots with their names; the host is marked. Rooms hold **2 players** (Quick Battle is 1v1).
- Each player picks their fighter with the existing fighter picker and comfort/loadout rules, reusing `CharacterSelectScreen` / `LocalFighterScreen` patterns. **The stage is chosen at random** (reuse `randomStage` from `js/screens/quick-battle-setup.js`), using a seed that the host sends, so both players get the same stage. Show which stage was drawn before the match starts.
- Both players press **Ready**; the host's client starts the match when both are ready.
- After the match: **Rematch** (both must accept; draw a new random stage) or **Leave room**. If the host leaves, close the room and tell the other player ("The host closed the room.").

### 1.5 Online battle rules

- It is the normal Quick Battle match: same clock, overtime, points, Void, HUD and results.
- **There is no pause online.** Options / Menu (and the HUD's pause button) opens a menu with only **Leave match** that does **not** freeze the world. Leaving counts as a forfeit, and the opponent sees "Your opponent left."
- **Opponent disconnects mid-match:** show "Waiting for opponent…" for up to 10 seconds, then end the match ("Connection lost") and return both players to the room or lobby.
- HUD labels show the players' online names instead of P1/P2. The local player is always shown on their own side. Marker colours come from a new `CONFIG.online.colors`, or reuse the Local Play colours.
- The local player uses their usual input: keyboard, touch controls or any controller. The single-player input path works unchanged.

### 1.6 "Servers are full"

- The server enforces a **global cap** so the free Cloudflare plan is never exceeded (see §3.4). When the cap is reached, creating a room is refused with code `full`. The game shows a dialog: "Servers are full right now. Please try again in a few minutes." (FR equivalent) and stays in the lobby.
- If the server can't be reached at all (down, not deployed yet, or blocked), show: "Online play is unavailable right now." This is a different message from "full".

