## 3. The room server (Cloudflare Workers + Durable Objects)

### 3.1 Where it lives

- Create `server/` at the repo root with its own `package.json` (devDependency `wrangler`), `wrangler.toml`, `src/index.js`, and tests.
- Add `server/` to the itch release's development-only exclusions (`DEVELOPMENT_ONLY` in `tools/itch/validate.mjs`, and the build), so none of it ships in the itch ZIP.
- Plain JavaScript (ES modules), no TypeScript and no frameworks, to match the repo.

### 3.2 Design

- A Worker handles HTTP and WebSocket upgrades. It routes to:
  - one **`Lobby` Durable Object** (a single named instance) that knows every open room: id, code, name, host name, public/private, mode, player count, status, created/last-active times. It hands out codes, serves the public list, and enforces the caps.
  - one **`Room` Durable Object per room**, holding the two players' WebSockets. It relays only lobby and room messages (join, leave, ready, fighter picked, start, rematch, WebRTC signalling) and reports changes back to the Lobby.
- Use the **WebSocket Hibernation API** (`state.acceptWebSocket`) so idle rooms don't use Durable Object duration.
- Endpoints, all JSON:
  - `GET /health`
  - `GET /rooms` (public, joinable rooms)
  - `POST /rooms` (create: name, playerName, visibility, mode → id, code, or `{ error: 'full' }` with HTTP 503)
  - `POST /rooms/join` (by code, or by id for public rooms)
  - `GET /rooms/:id/ws` (the WebSocket into a room)
- Validate every field on the server (lengths, characters, mode is in the allowed list). Reject anything else with clear error codes the client translates (`invalid_name`, `room_not_found`, `room_full`, `full`, `rate_limited`).
- **CORS:** allow only itch's game origins (`https://html-classic.itch.zone`, `https://html.itch.zone`, and any other `*.itch.zone` origin itch uses; check itch's docs) plus `http://localhost:*` for development. Put the allowed origins in `wrangler.toml` vars so the owner can change them without code. Check the `Origin` header on WebSocket upgrades too.
- Clean up rooms with no activity for 10 minutes, and rooms whose host left, using a Durable Object alarm.
- Rate-limit per IP (e.g. 10 room creations per minute) in the Lobby, keeping counters in memory or storage. Don't log IPs persistently.

### 3.3 No accounts, no personal data

- No login, cookies or stored identity. Each client generates a random session id per visit to recognise reconnects. Names live only while the room exists.

### 3.4 Staying under the free plan

Cloudflare's free plan allows **100,000 Durable Object requests per day** (WebSocket connections count as requests; incoming WebSocket messages count at 20:1) and **13,000 GB-s of duration per day**; the daily reset is at 00:00 UTC. Check the current numbers in the Cloudflare docs before you write the caps, since they may have changed.

- `MAX_ROOMS` (start at 50) open at once. Over it, `POST /rooms` returns `full`.
- A **daily request budget** counted in the Lobby (count requests/messages handled, reset at 00:00 UTC). Above ~80% of the daily limit, refuse new rooms with `full` while letting existing rooms finish.
- Both caps go in `wrangler.toml` vars, with the reasoning written as a comment next to them.
- itch.io itself imposes no player limit on HTML5 games; the caps protect the Cloudflare free plan.

### 3.5 Testing the server without an account

- Write unit tests for the Lobby and Room logic as plain classes with fake storage/WebSockets, runnable with `node --test`. Keep the main repo's test command working: either include them in it or document the separate command and add it to the GitHub workflow.
- Document how to run it locally with `npx wrangler dev`, which needs no Cloudflare account, and point the game at `http://localhost:8787` via config for a two-tab local test.

